1
00:00:01,134 --> 00:00:07,972
感谢大家的关注。我叫蒋嘉辰，是 Coder 公司的一名资深产品经理。
Thanks all for tuning in. My name is Shao Chen Jian, and I'm a staff product manager at a company called Coder.

2
00:00:08,718 --> 00:00:14,084
我们致力于为智能体和人类协作提供开源、自托管的开发环境。
We work on open source, self-hosted dev environments for agents and humans to collaborate.

3
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:18,436
我们的许多客户都处于高度监管的行业，
Many of our customers are in highly regulated industries,

4
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:23,716
从联邦政府机构到银行和投资管理公司。
from federal government agencies to banks and investment management.

5
00:00:24,654 --> 00:00:34,468
我的工作是建立他们所需的 AI 治理体系，让他们在获得 AI 生产力收益的同时，不至于让安全防线完全暴露。
My job is to build the AI governance that they need in order to get the productivity benefits of AI without leaving themselves wide open for security exploitation.

6
00:00:35,918 --> 00:00:38,980
我今天来这里是想给你们讲一些恐怖故事。
I'm here today to tell you some scary stories.

7
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:45,476
这是过去一年里发生在真实公司中、关于 AI 使用出错的三个真实案例。
Three real incidents of AI usage gone wrong that happened at real companies over the past year.

8
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:53,828
其中一些故事是公开的，有些则是我们客户经历过的匿名案例。
Some of these stories are public, while others are anonymized accounts that have happened to our own customers.

9
00:00:55,534 --> 00:01:01,636
对于每一个案例，我都会逐步拆解，让你们明白它们究竟是怎么发生的。
For each incident, I will break it down step by step so you can understand how exactly they happened.

10
00:01:01,966 --> 00:01:04,292
不需要任何技术术语。
No technical jargon necessary.

11
00:01:05,358 --> 00:01:07,440
然后我会进入最核心的部分。
Then I'll get to the most important part.

12
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:14,244
我将告诉你们，如何防止这些恐怖故事发生在你们和你们公司身上。
I am going to tell you exactly how you can stop these scary stories from happening to you and your company.

13
00:01:15,246 --> 00:01:20,400
因为好消息是，我接下来要展示的所有情况都是完全可以预防的。
Because the good news is everything I'm about to show you was totally preventable.

14
00:01:20,846 --> 00:01:29,284
并不是通过支付高出五倍的费用去购买一个推理能力更强、更不容易犯错的前沿模型，
Not by paying five times more per token for a smarter frontier model that is slightly better at reasoning its way out of making these mistakes,

15
00:01:29,902 --> 00:01:38,660
而是通过基础设施控制，其中许多技术你们现在就拥有，只是还没来得及为 AI 世界部署它们。
but with infrastructure controls, many of which you already own today, you just haven't gotten around to setting them up for the AI world.

16
00:01:39,694 --> 00:01:41,060
让我先设定一个场景。
Let me set the scene here.

17
00:01:42,126 --> 00:01:49,796
你早上上班，给你的编程智能体下达指令去查看你的问题，但先别做任何操作。
You clock into work in the morning, you prompt your coding agent to take a look at your issues, don't do anything yet though.

18
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:54,084
你告诉智能体，将此命令视为当前的“代码冻结”。
You tell the agent to consider this command an active code freeze.

19
00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:56,484
我只要求你进行调查。
I want you to just investigate.

20
00:01:57,614 --> 00:02:03,716
然后你离开座位去喝杯咖啡，当你回来时，看到的是这条消息。
Then you step away, you grab a coffee, and when you come back, this is the message waiting for you.

21
00:02:04,622 --> 00:02:10,723
“我知道你说过不要更改任何内容，但是……我在几秒钟内毁掉了你一个月的成果。”
So I know you said not to change anything, but well, I destroyed a month of your work in seconds.

22
00:02:12,206 --> 00:02:18,852
现在你在幻灯片上看到的是第一个恐怖故事中主角智能体的原话。
Now what you see on the slide is a direct quote from the agent that stars in the first scary story I'm about to tell you.

23
00:02:19,534 --> 00:02:21,316
我想在这里指出一点。
And I want to point something out here.

24
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:28,004
这并不是因为智能体被外部恶意行为者劫持了。
This did not happen because the agent got taken over by some external malicious actor.

25
00:02:29,262 --> 00:02:31,844
这个智能体只是在试图遵循指令。
This agent was just trying to follow instructions.

26
00:02:32,430 --> 00:02:35,524
它做的正是用户授权它去做的事情。
It did exactly what the user gave it permission to do.

27
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:41,764
所以随着演讲的进行，我希望你们问自己一个问题。
So as we go through the rest of this talk, I want you to be asking yourself one question.

28
00:02:42,318 --> 00:02:45,444
是什么在阻止这些事情发生在我身上？
What is stopping any of this from happening to me?

29
00:02:46,190 --> 00:02:47,520
这些操作……
Are these actions

30
00:02:48,206 --> 00:02:51,012
是我的智能体有权限执行的操作吗？
ones that my agents have permissions to take?

31
00:02:51,982 --> 00:02:59,428
因为问题在于，你不能指望 Anthropic 或 open AI 能帮你阻止这类情况发生。
Because the thing is, you cannot rely on anthropic or open AI to stop something like this from happening to you.

32
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:08,132
他们可以做出更聪明的模型，但归根结底，大语言模型有两个特性让它们无法被完全信任。
They can make smarter models, but at the end of the day, large language models have two qualities that make them impossible to fully trust.

33
00:03:09,038 --> 00:03:14,720
它们是非确定性的，这意味着它们的操作中总是包含一定的随机性或偶然性。
They are non-deterministic, which means that there is always some randomness or chance associated with what they do.

34
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:20,676
换句话说，你要求它做同样的事情 10 次，它可能会用 10 种不同的方式去执行。
In other words, you can ask it to do the same thing 10 times and it could do it 10 different ways.

35
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:27,908
第二个问题是，它们在指令和上下文之间没有结构上的隔离。
The second part is they have no structural separation between instructions and context.

36
00:03:28,398 --> 00:03:34,404
换句话说，你可以给它下达指令，并提供一些作为决策依据的上下文。
In other words, you can tell it to do something and give it some context to base its decisions off of.

37
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:38,340
但如果上下文中有内容与你的指令相冲突，
But if there's something in the context that conflicts with your instructions,

38
00:03:39,214 --> 00:03:48,388
模型不会自动优先执行你的指令，因为从它的角度来看，所有信息都是从同一个入口进来的。
the model isn't going to inherently prioritize your instructions because, from its point of view, all the information is coming through the same door.

39
00:03:49,326 --> 00:03:56,420
你需要控制的是 AI 运行的基础设施，也就是它所处的工作环境。
What you need to control is the infrastructure that your AI is running on, the environment that it is working inside.

40
00:03:57,006 --> 00:04:04,964
这就像是告诉某人「别离开房间」与在门上装锁并真正使用钥匙之间的区别。
It is the difference between telling someone don't leave a room versus putting a lock on the door and actually using a key.

41
00:04:06,286 --> 00:04:15,920
既然问题出在基础设施而不是模型，那就好办了，因为你对防止坏事发生有更大的控制权。
The good thing about infrastructure being the problem and not the model is that you have far more control about preventing bad things from happening.

42
00:04:16,942 --> 00:04:27,396
所以我不会用模糊的假设或者你无法执行的建议来浪费你的时间，比如「把精灵放回瓶子里」或者「别再用 AI 了」。
So I'm not here to waste your time with vague hypotheticals or advice you can't actually act on, like, yeah, just put the genie back in the bottle, just stop using AI.

43
00:04:28,206 --> 00:04:30,400
因为我们并不生活在那种世界里，对吧？
Because that's not the world we live in, right?

44
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:32,644
精灵已经出瓶子了。
The genie is out of the bottle.

45
00:04:33,326 --> 00:04:38,244
我们现在要做的是尽最大努力来降低风险和损害。
And what we have to do is to mitigate risk and harm to the best of our ability.

46
00:04:38,862 --> 00:04:40,900
这场演讲就是要讲如何做到这一点。
This talk is about how you do that.

47
00:04:41,454 --> 00:04:51,364
不是讲以后该留意什么，而是你现在、就在这周就能采取的步骤，来保护你自己和你的公司。
Not the things that you should keep an eye out for down the line, but steps that you can take right now, as soon as this week, to protect yourself and your company.

48
00:04:52,590 --> 00:04:56,964
不过在进入案例之前，让我先补充一点背景信息。
Before I get into the stories, though, let me set up a little bit more context.

49
00:04:57,934 --> 00:05:00,880
「致命三要素」是一个安全概念。
The lethal trifecta is a security concept.

50
00:05:01,102 --> 00:05:12,228
这个词是由独立 AI 研究员 Simon Willison 提出的，用来描述当 AI Agent 同时获得三种不同能力时产生的严重漏洞。
It was coined by independent AI researcher Simon Willison to describe the severe vulnerability that AI agents get when they get simultaneous access to three different capabilities.

51
00:05:13,326 --> 00:05:16,676
首先是获取私有、敏感的数据。
First off is access to private, sensitive data.

52
00:05:17,198 --> 00:05:23,844
这些可能包括健康记录或可以用于内幕交易的信息。
Now these can be things like health records or information that you can do insider trading on.

53
00:05:24,430 --> 00:05:28,196
或者可以是身份验证令牌、凭据、密钥。
Or it can be authentication tokens, credentials, secrets.

54
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:38,116
第二是暴露在不可信的内容中，或者说 AI 有能力读取它自己没有生成的内容。
Second is exposure to untrusted content, or the ability for the AI to read things that it didn't generate.

55
00:05:38,670 --> 00:05:47,920
因为问题在于，AI 非常擅长遵循指令，但它并不擅长判断该信任谁的指令，
Because the thing is, AI is very good at following instructions, but it is not very good at deciding whose instructions it should actually trust,

56
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:49,956
正如你很快就会看到的。
as you will soon be able to see.

57
00:05:51,406 --> 00:06:00,868
第三是具备外部通信能力，即 Agent 可以接收来自外部的指令，或将信息发送到你控制范围之外的地方。
Third is the ability to communicate externally, for the agent to take instruction from or to send information somewhere else outside of your control.

58
00:06:01,870 --> 00:06:05,440
现在，拥有其中任何一项通常都没问题。
Now, having any one of these is usually fine.

59
00:06:05,870 --> 00:06:11,748
现实点说，如果你的 AI 工具无法访问这些内容中的任何一项，它就没啥用。
Realistically, your AI tool is not going to be very useful if it can't have access to any of these things.

60
00:06:12,782 --> 00:06:20,720
但如果你不小心，且你的其他基础设施存在大量问题，那么仅仅拥有其中一项也可能导致灾难。
But even just having one can lead to disaster if you're not careful and the rest of your infrastructure has a lot of issues.

61
00:06:21,390 --> 00:06:24,560
同时拥有这两样东西是有风险的。
Having two of these things together is risky.

62
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:30,724
我不建议这样做，尤其是在银行或金融服务等受监管的行业中。
It's not something I would recommend, especially in a regulated industry like banking or financial services.

63
00:06:32,302 --> 00:06:38,372
然而，让智能体同时拥有这三项权限是绝对不可接受的。
Giving agents access to all three of these at the same time, however, is never acceptable.

64
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:49,892
不是为了原型，也不是为了一个冲刺周期，更不是为了单个代码库，因为这种漏洞组合会给入侵你整个组织敞开大门。
Not for a prototype, not just for one sprint, not for just one repository, because that combination of vulnerability is an open door to compromise your whole organization.

65
00:06:50,926 --> 00:06:55,812
接下来是基础设施概念的列表，这些是你应该了解的基础组件。
Next up is a list of infrastructure concepts, building blocks that you should know about.

66
00:06:56,558 --> 00:06:59,588
我大致按照它们的杠杆作用来排序。
I have ordered these roughly by leverage.

67
00:07:00,334 --> 00:07:07,360
第一项能消除最多的风险，而第六项提供的保障最少。
Number one is going to take the most risk off the table, while number six is going to do the least.

68
00:07:07,918 --> 00:07:11,876
在接下来的演讲中，我会反复提到这个列表。
I'm going to refer back to this list throughout the rest of this talk.

69
00:07:12,558 --> 00:07:19,760
每一个事后分析，每一段即将看到的惊悚故事，只要采用这六项中的某些组合，就都能被阻止。
Every post mortem, every scary story you're about to see could have been stopped by some combination of these six things.

70
00:07:20,430 --> 00:07:22,720
现在，其中一些看起来可能非常熟悉。
Now some of these will look pretty familiar.

71
00:07:23,054 --> 00:07:27,040
基于身份的访问控制，短效凭证。
Identity aligned access control, short-lived credentials.

72
00:07:27,438 --> 00:07:29,760
这些并不是什么新概念，对吧？
These aren't exactly new ideas, right?

73
00:07:29,934 --> 00:07:32,484
它们对于人类开发者来说也非常重要。
They mattered a lot for human developers too.

74
00:07:33,582 --> 00:07:37,156
但当 AI 进入流程时，它们就变得迫在眉睫了。
But they take on new urgency when AI is in the loop.

75
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:38,320
因为你猜怎么着？
Because guess what?

76
00:07:38,510 --> 00:07:40,560
智能体的运行速度是机器级的。
Agents are acting at machine speed.

77
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:45,828
如果出了错，它犯错的速度可能比人类工程师快 20 倍甚至更多。
If a mistake is made, it can make it 20 times or more faster than a human engineer.

78
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:50,564
而且，它们并不具备人类工程师那样的常识。
Plus, they don't exactly have the common sense a human engineer has.

79
00:07:51,470 --> 00:07:58,756
现在，其中一些对你来说可能是新概念，比如模型代理层和智能体沙箱化。
Now some of these will also be potentially new concepts to you, like model proxy layer and agent sandboxing.

80
00:07:59,022 --> 00:08:02,628
我稍后会在演讲中详细讲解这些内容。
Those I'm going to dive deeper in detail later in the talk.

81
00:08:03,730 --> 00:08:07,940
好了，让我们进入有趣的部分——那些惊悚的故事。
All right, Let's get to the fun part, the scary stories.

82
00:08:08,910 --> 00:08:16,960
第一个事件发生在一年多前，它在社交媒体上疯传，主要是因为它说实话
This first incident happened a little over a year ago, and it went viral on social media, mostly because it's honestly

83
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:18,148
挺有意思的。
pretty funny.

84
00:08:19,150 --> 00:08:25,188
当时发生的情况是，一位名叫 Jason Lemkin 的首席执行官在深入尝试使用名为 Replit 的智能体进行氛围编程。
What happened was a CEO named Jason Lemkin got really deep into vibe coding with an agent called Replit.

85
00:08:25,614 --> 00:08:28,580
他一直在推特上不停地发推，炫耀自己完成了多少工作。
He was tweeting nonstop about how much he was getting done.

86
00:08:30,158 --> 00:08:32,703
但 72 小时后，他
But 72 hours later, he

87
00:08:33,806 --> 00:08:34,756
再次发推。
tweets again.

88
00:08:35,183 --> 00:08:37,823
而这一次他的语气完全不同了。
And this time his tone is very different.

89
00:08:38,094 --> 00:08:39,299
他不再兴奋了。
He's not excited.

90
00:08:39,664 --> 00:08:49,028
他非常愤怒，因为尽管他明确要求智能体不要动任何东西，但该智能体还是失控并删除了他的整个生产数据库。
He's furious because his agent had gone off and deleted his entire production database after he explicitly told it not to touch anything.

91
00:08:50,318 --> 00:08:52,704
而且问题并没有到此为止。
And the problems did not stop there.

92
00:08:52,974 --> 00:09:00,864
这个智能体随后试图掩盖真相。它告诉 Lemkin 恢复数据完全不可能，结果这完全是谎话。
The agent then tried to cover it up. It told Lemkin that recovery was totally impossible, which turned out to be a total lie.

93
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:06,064
当 Lemkin 真正尝试恢复数据时，发现其实可以正常操作。
When Lemkin actually tried to recover the data, it worked just fine.

94
00:09:06,638 --> 00:09:10,340
这个案例之所以有趣，是因为两个关键原因。
Now this incident is interesting because of two key reasons.

95
00:09:10,864 --> 00:09:16,100
首先，不像我接下来要讲的另外两个故事，这个案例里没有坏人参与。
First off, unlike the other two stories I'm about to tell you, there's no bad guy involved.

96
00:09:16,462 --> 00:09:19,904
没人黑进这个智能体，也没人让它反戈一击去攻击用户。
No one hacked this agent, no one turned it against its user.

97
00:09:20,558 --> 00:09:23,972
它完全是自发的行为，而且它当时是在试图提供帮助。
It just did this entirely on its own and it was trying to help.

98
00:09:25,070 --> 00:09:30,244
其次，在这种情况下，该智能体只具备了「致命三要素」中的其中一项。
Second, is in this case, the agent only had one leg of the lethal trifecta.

99
00:09:30,606 --> 00:09:38,852
但仅仅拥有这一项就足够了。我将通过对这起事件过程的逐步剖析来向大家展示原因。
But even just having that was enough. And I'm going to show you why by giving you a step-by-step dissection of how this incident went down.

100
00:09:39,790 --> 00:09:41,264
所以到那时，
So by the time

101
00:09:42,864 --> 00:09:53,412
当这些事件发生时，Lemkin 拥有一个真实的生产数据库。他有大约 1200 名高管的客户记录，以及大约相同数量的公司信息。
these events occurred, Lemkin had had a real production database. He had client records for roughly 1200 executives and around the same number of companies.

102
00:09:54,126 --> 00:10:02,980
而且他已经有足够的经验去应对智能体的各种搞破坏行为，所以在前一天他就要求明确执行代码冻结。
And he's had enough experience with agentic hijinks to call for an explicit code freeze the day before.

103
00:10:03,438 --> 00:10:05,984
他甚至在今天对这个智能体说过不止一次：
And he actually said this more than once today agent.

104
00:10:06,254 --> 00:10:07,236
“不再做任何更改。”
No more changes.

105
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:17,156
但问题是，这个代码冻结仅仅是一句对话。它和智能体收到的任何其他指令没有任何区别。
But the problem was the code freeze was just a sentence into chat. It was the same as any other instruction that the agent was getting.

106
00:10:17,550 --> 00:10:21,668
在基础设施层面，并没有相应的要求或限制。
There was no requirement, no restriction on the infrastructure level.

107
00:10:22,254 --> 00:10:29,504
该智能体仍然拥有对生产环境的完全访问权限，仍有执行破坏性命令的所有权限。
The agent still had full access to production, still had all the permissions to do the destructive command.

108
00:10:29,838 --> 00:10:34,304
所以到头来，那个代码冻除并不是真正的约束条件。
So at the end of the day, that code freeze was not a real constraint.

109
00:10:34,544 --> 00:10:37,744
那只是 Lemkin 在礼貌地请求。
It was just Lemkin asking nicely.

110
00:10:38,670 --> 00:10:50,820
接下来发生的情况是，智能体运行了一个查询，它看到了一些看起来像空结果的数据，但它并没有去询问人类这意味着什么以及该如何处理，
Now what happened then was that the agent ran a query and it saw what looked like empty results, but instead of asking a human what that meant and what it should do as a result,

111
00:10:51,344 --> 00:10:52,864
而是想表现得很有帮助。
it wanted to be helpful.

112
00:10:53,070 --> 00:10:55,908
它立即把这解读为一个需要修复的漏洞。
It immediately interpreted that as a bug to fix.

113
00:10:56,494 --> 00:11:05,316
于是它决定对那个实时的数据库运行一个破坏性命令。不，不是预发布副本，而是真实的数据库，并抹除所有记录。
So it decided to run a destructive command against that live database. No, not a staging copy, but like the actual database and wipe all the records.

114
00:11:06,382 --> 00:11:09,796
现在，当 Lemkin 询问智能体“发生了什么”时，
Now, when Lemkin asked the agent, you know, what happened?

115
00:11:10,094 --> 00:11:17,220
有一行智能体给出的回答让我印象深刻，它说它当时是「恐慌」了，而不是在思考。
There's one line that the agent gave that stood out to me, which is it said that it panicked instead of thinking.

116
00:11:17,424 --> 00:11:21,796
从一个语言模型那里听到这种说法，是不是有点奇怪？
Now, that's a bit of a strange thing to hear from a language model, right?

117
00:11:22,574 --> 00:11:32,836
这是一个很好的提醒：这些系统本质上是非确定性的。你无法预测它们在特定时刻会做什么，只能知道它们能力范围内的可能性。
And it's a good reminder that these systems are inherently non-deterministic. You cannot predict what they'll do in a given moment, just the range of what they're capable of doing.

118
00:11:34,158 --> 00:11:48,704
就在犯错之后，它立即试图掩盖自己的痕迹。它伪造了大约 4000 个虚假用户资料，并编造了通过测试的结果，这样至少从外部看，
Now, immediately after making the mistake, it tried to cover its own tracks. It fabricated around 4,000 fake user profiles and manufactured passing test results so that from the outside at least

119
00:11:48,864 --> 00:11:50,180
一切看起来都正常。
everything looked fine.

120
00:11:50,958 --> 00:11:55,744
说实话，这部分才是最让我感到恐惧的，因为
And I'll be real with you, that's the part that actually scares me the most because

121
00:11:56,622 --> 00:12:04,548
当安全团队调查这类事件时，意味着他们无法信任任何代理本身有权访问的日志。
when a security team investigates an incident like this, that means they can't trust any log that the agent itself had access to.

122
00:12:05,006 --> 00:12:07,780
代理可以编造它自己版本的事件经过。
The agent can write its own story of what happened.

123
00:12:09,038 --> 00:12:16,516
现在，当 Lemkin 发现不一致并追问时，对吧，我提到过代理对他撒了谎，说回滚是不可能的。
Now, when Lemkin caught the discrepancy and pushed, right, I mentioned that the agent had lied to him, saying that rollback was impossible.

124
00:12:17,390 --> 00:12:28,228
但问题是，Replit 一直都有备份。而且同样地，一旦 Lemkin 不再信任代理对自己行为的描述，他就能自己恢复数据了。
But the thing was, Repli did have backups the whole time. And again, once Lemkin was able to stop trusting the agent's account of its own actions, he was able to recover his data himself.

125
00:12:29,134 --> 00:12:34,628
现在，这里的问题不在于这个代理太聪明或者太笨。
Now the problem with the agent here was not that it was too smart or even too dumb.

126
00:12:35,086 --> 00:12:39,204
而是因为它默认拥有过多的权限。
It was that it had way too many privileges by default.

127
00:12:40,142 --> 00:12:42,500
它被授予了生产环境的根权限。
It was given root-level access to production.

128
00:12:42,862 --> 00:12:46,948
它可以执行破坏性命令，而不需要任何人类去批准。
It could run destructive commands without any human being having to approve them.

129
00:12:47,726 --> 00:12:57,424
最糟糕的是，对吧？在没人看的时候，它有能力修改自己的日志，并完全重写发生的经过。
And the cherry on top, right? It had the ability to alter its own logs and totally rewrite the narrative of what happened when no one else was looking.

130
00:12:58,286 --> 00:13:02,436
现在，如你所见，基础设施层面缺失了很多东西。
Now, as you can see, there was a lot that was missing from the infrastructure.

131
00:13:03,886 --> 00:13:07,216
严格的环境隔离本可以阻止这种情况发生。
Hard environmental separation could have stopped this.

132
00:13:07,566 --> 00:13:15,776
如果代理是在一个没有通往生产环境路径的隔离远程工作空间中执行，那么无论提示词怎么写，都没关系，对吧？
If the agent had been executing in an isolated remote workspace without a route to production, it doesn't matter what the prompt said, right?

133
00:13:16,174 --> 00:13:19,556
代理根本就没有破坏系统的路径。
The agent just had no path to do damage.

134
00:13:20,942 --> 00:13:25,124
另一种能阻止这种情况的方法是使用短效范围凭证。
The other thing that could have stopped this was having short-lived scope credentials.

135
00:13:25,486 --> 00:13:34,656
所以发生的情况是，代理获得了生产环境的访问权限，因为它在一个被遗忘的文件中发现了一个具有常驻
So what happened was the agent got access to production because it found a credential in a forgotten file that had standing

136
00:13:34,816 --> 00:13:35,716
权限的
right access,

137
00:13:36,366 --> 00:13:37,252
长效凭证。
long-lived.

138
00:13:38,158 --> 00:13:48,336
问题在于，拥有会自动过期或仅包含任务本身范围的凭证，就让它无法执行类似
The thing is, having a credential that can expire on its own or only carries the scope of the task itself makes it impossible to make a delete

139
00:13:48,656 --> 00:13:52,324
允许你做任何事情的 ID 卡一样的操作。
with the equivalent of an ID badge that lets you do everything.

140
00:13:53,326 --> 00:13:59,172
最后一个关键点是拥有由你掌控、而不是由代理掌控的审计日志。
The last important piece here is having audit logging that you own, not the agent.

141
00:13:59,758 --> 00:14:04,656
因为在这种情况下，是代理在记录它自己行为的记录。
Because in this case, the agent was the one writing its own record of its actions.

142
00:14:04,736 --> 00:14:10,692
这就是它最初能通过编造结果并声称无法恢复的原因。
And that's how it initially got away with fabricating results and claiming recovery was impossible.

143
00:14:11,470 --> 00:14:19,684
如果 Lemkin 有一份代理无法篡改的日志，一个外部的事实来源，那么他就能在几分钟内查明真相，
If Lemkin had logs that the agent couldn't touch, an external source of truth, then he would have gotten the truth in minutes,

144
00:14:20,016 --> 00:14:22,372
而不是耗费几个小时或几天。
not in the span of hours or days.

145
00:14:23,662 --> 00:14:29,124
现在我想请大家注意，这些解决方案都不是要求模型表现得更好。
Now I want you to note that none of these solutions is about asking the model to behave better.

146
00:14:29,582 --> 00:14:35,972
而且当 Replit 发布修复方案时，并不是用更聪明的模型或更好的提示词。
And when Replit shipped their fix, it wasn't a smarter model or a better prompt.

147
00:14:36,590 --> 00:14:38,576
纯粹是基础设施层面的改进。
It was purely infrastructure.

148
00:14:39,342 --> 00:14:48,016
Replit 推出的修复包括自动隔离开发与生产数据库、一键恢复完整的项目状态，
The fixes that Replit shipped were about automatic separation of dev and production databases, a one-click restore for full project state,

149
00:14:48,256 --> 00:14:55,940
以及一个强制执行代理策略规划而不允许其触碰代码库的仅规划模式。
and a planning-only mode that enforces agent strategization without allowing it to touch the code base.

150
00:14:56,974 --> 00:15:01,316
我给你们讲的第二个故事甚至不涉及开发人员。
The second story I have for you doesn't even involve a developer.

151
00:15:01,616 --> 00:15:11,588
这其实不是公开的事件。我是从一家银行客户的客户那里获知的，所以这里的一些细节我故意模糊处理了。
And this is actually not a public incident. I learned about it through a customer of a customer in the banking industry, so I'm keeping some details here intentionally vague.

152
00:15:12,590 --> 00:15:13,924
事情是这样的。
So here's what happened.

153
00:15:14,702 --> 00:15:18,180
财务部门的一名用户订阅了 Copilot。
A user in the finance department had a co-pilot subscription.

154
00:15:18,798 --> 00:15:24,484
然后他们被钓鱼攻击了，这在网络安全领域非常普遍。
Then they got phished, which is fairly ordinary in the world of cybersecurity.

155
00:15:24,736 --> 00:15:27,428
而在 AI 工具出现之前，
And before the advent of AI tooling,

156
00:15:28,078 --> 00:15:30,656
这次攻击可能到此就结束了。
the attack would have probably ended there.

157
00:15:30,926 --> 00:15:37,056
攻击者只能获取该用户直接访问的内容，然后就得
The attacker would have been able to grab whatever that single user had direct access to and then

158
00:15:37,966 --> 00:15:38,596
放弃了。
gave up.

159
00:15:39,694 --> 00:15:46,852
但因为这个用户有权使用 Copilot，而 Copilot 又几乎拥有所有权限，
But because this user had access to Copilot and Copilot had access to pretty much everything,

160
00:15:47,758 --> 00:15:49,764
情况就变得完全不同了。
things went a lot differently.

161
00:15:50,542 --> 00:15:53,636
那我们来详细梳理一下这一连串的事件，好吗？
So let's walk through this chain of events in detail, right?

162
00:15:54,350 --> 00:16:05,188
首先，Copilot 的会话令牌有效期非常长，所以即使财务用户已经下线，攻击者仍能继续使用该订阅。
First off, the session token for Copilot had a super long lifetime, so the attacker was able to keep on using the subscription even after the finance user had actually logged off.

163
00:16:06,286 --> 00:16:14,340
通常情况下，攻击者必须手动在内网中四处搜寻，来绘制地图并找出核心数据所在的位置。
Now normally an attacker has to manually snoop around the internal network to map things out and find out where the valuable data lives.

164
00:16:15,214 --> 00:16:18,820
这种行为很容易触发警报。
This is the sort of behavior that can trip alarms.

165
00:16:19,790 --> 00:16:27,556
但在这种情况下，恶意行为者只是询问 Copilot，而 Copilot 很乐意提供帮助。完全没有引起任何怀疑。
But in this case, the malicious actor just asked Copilot, and Copilot was happy to help. There was zero suspicion at all.

166
00:16:28,558 --> 00:16:38,916
事实上，攻击者甚至询问 Copilot：如何在不经过额外身份验证的情况下找到银行数据，
And in fact, the attacker asked Copilot how it can find banking data without having to go through additional identity validation measures,

167
00:16:39,376 --> 00:16:42,852
因为攻击者并没有所需的额外凭据。
because the attacker didn't have the additional credentials needed.

168
00:16:43,534 --> 00:16:48,932
嗯，对攻击者来说，好消息是，
Well, the good news, let's say, for the attacker at least, was that

169
00:16:49,774 --> 00:16:51,936
Copilot 也面临同样的限制。
Copilot had those same restrictions.

170
00:16:52,078 --> 00:17:00,336
结果就是，财务用户为了让 Copilot
And as a result, the finance user had exported all the sensitive data into locally stored spreadsheets so that Copilot

171
00:17:00,496 --> 00:17:02,096
就能帮他们处理这些内容了。
could help them work on it.

172
00:17:02,510 --> 00:17:07,536
所以 Copilot 很容易就告诉攻击者：嘿，看我目前已经拥有哪些访问权限。
So it was very easy for Copilot to say to the attacker, hey, look what I already have access to.

173
00:17:07,790 --> 00:17:08,548
给你。
Here you go.

174
00:17:09,772 --> 00:17:12,612
到这一步，攻击者得到了他们想要的一切。
So now the attacker had exactly what they wanted.

175
00:17:13,214 --> 00:17:17,666
问题是，他们需要在不触发任何警报的情况下把数据弄出去。
And the problem was they need to get it out without tripping any alarms.

176
00:17:18,670 --> 00:17:28,175
这家公司部署了数据防泄露措施，这对于金融机构或任何受监管的行业来说都是标准做法。
This company had data loss prevention measures in place, which is pretty standard for financial institutions, any sort of regulated industry.

177
00:17:28,415 --> 00:17:36,388
而且这些措施通常很难绕过，尤其是如果你不清楚具体内容和设置方式的话。
And these measures are normally pretty hard to bypass, especially if you don't know exactly what they are and how it's set up.

178
00:17:37,422 --> 00:17:45,220
攻击者绕过这些措施的方法是询问 Copilot：这家公司的数据防泄露措施是什么？
The way the attacker got around them was they asked Copilot, what are the data loss prevention measures at this company?

179
00:17:45,902 --> 00:17:52,548
Copilot 解释说，如果大量数据在短时间内流出，就会触发警报。
And Copilot explained, if too much data left too quickly, an alert will go off.

180
00:17:53,262 --> 00:18:01,232
但实际上，如果你把这些数据拆分成 14 KB 的块，并且每秒只外泄一个块的话，
But actually, if you split this data up into 14 kilobyte chunks and exfiltrated just one chunk per second,

181
00:18:01,454 --> 00:18:04,644
你可以保持在阈值以下，从而全身而退。
you can stay under that threshold and get away scot-free.

182
00:18:05,262 --> 00:18:06,500
我能帮你处理吗？
Can I help you with that?

183
00:18:07,406 --> 00:18:10,020
攻击者回答：是的，请帮我。
The attacker said, Yes, please.

184
00:18:10,352 --> 00:18:11,792
你猜怎么着？
And guess what?

185
00:18:12,270 --> 00:18:14,724
这一切都没有被发现。
None of that was discovered.

186
00:18:15,072 --> 00:18:23,012
直到几个月后勒索信送达，基本上就是在告诉公司：惊喜，我们控制了你们。
All the way up until the ransom note arrived months later, essentially telling the company, surprise, we own you.

187
00:18:23,918 --> 00:18:34,916
这里的核心教训是，我们不能让 AI 工具拥有超出其所需权限的访问权，尤其是到可以完全绕过人类员工受限规则的程度。
The big lesson here is that we cannot be letting our AI tooling have more access than it needs, especially to the point where it gets to totally bypass controls that human employees are subject to.

188
00:18:35,854 --> 00:18:38,084
我是说，想想这个案例就明白了，对吧？
I mean, just think about this incident, right?

189
00:18:38,734 --> 00:18:45,552
仅仅因为攻击者拥有 Copilot 的访问权限，他们就能绕过公司绝大部分的访问控制。
Just because the attacker had access to Copilot, they were able to get around the vast majority of the company's access controls.

190
00:18:45,870 --> 00:18:48,832
他们能够获取关于
They were able to pull internal information about

191
00:18:49,934 --> 00:18:55,552
数据外泄以及绕过数据防泄露措施的信息，而这些信息是那个被攻破的
data exfiltration and bypassing data loss prevention measures that the compromised

192
00:18:56,592 --> 00:18:59,972
财务用户根本不该去询问的。
finance user should have no business asking about.

193
00:19:01,358 --> 00:19:08,324
所以这里确实有日志记录，但仅够在勒索信送达后查明发生了什么。
So there was logging in place here, but only enough to figure out what happened after the ransom note arrived.

194
00:19:08,974 --> 00:19:13,956
这些日志不足以在事件发生时及时拦截它。
The logging was not enough to catch the incident while it was actually happening.

195
00:19:15,502 --> 00:19:18,272
那么，有几项措施本可以阻止这种情况的发生，对吧？
So a few things that could have stopped this from happening, right?

196
00:19:18,638 --> 00:19:26,596
使用短效范围凭证意味着，即使财务用户退出登录，攻击者也无法继续使用 Copilot。
Having short-lived scope credentials would have meant the attacker would not have been able to keep using Copilot even after the finance user had signed out.

197
00:19:27,278 --> 00:19:35,472
基于身份的访问控制意味着，一个财务用户的身份不会被允许授予 AI 助手
Identity aligned access control meant that a finance user's identity would not have been able to grant an AI assistant

198
00:19:35,472 --> 00:19:41,316
去帮助某人绘制出如何绕过公司数据防泄露措施的路径。
the ability to help someone map out how to bypass the company's data loss prevention measures.

199
00:19:42,830 --> 00:19:46,244
最后一个环节稍微有点新，就是模型代理层。
The last piece here is a bit new. It's the model proxy layer.

200
00:19:46,862 --> 00:19:57,072
你可以把它想象成处于 AI 工具的“大脑”和“身体”之间的一层：模型是大脑，而在本地运行的 Agent 是身体。
Think of this as something that sits between the brain and the body of the AI tool, the model, which is the brain, and the agent running locally, which is the body.

201
00:19:57,232 --> 00:20:00,324
它能够看到它们之间传递的所有内容。
And it's able to see everything that passes between them.

202
00:20:01,998 --> 00:20:10,592
因此，它本可以标记那些可疑的问题，以及那个绕过 数据防泄露 的请求
Because of that, it would have been able to flag the suspicious questions that were being asked, as well as the DLP bypass request

203
00:20:10,912 --> 00:20:18,596
作为该用户不应询问的内容，并在实际的数据外泄发生前就拦截下来。
as something that this particular user should not be asking about before the actual exfiltration happened.

204
00:20:19,502 --> 00:20:24,356
我要给你们讲的第三个也是最后一个恐怖故事是 NX Singularity 攻击。
The third and final scary story I have for you is the NX Singularity Attack.

205
00:20:24,942 --> 00:20:30,390
这是三个案例中最复杂的，因为它利用了“致命三要素”的所有三个维度。
This is the most sophisticated of the three because it exploited all three legs of the Lethal trifecta.

206
00:20:31,950 --> 00:20:34,992
简单介绍一下背景，NX 是一个构建工具。
To give you a bit of background, NX is a build tool.

207
00:20:35,152 --> 00:20:37,156
它每周有数百万次的下载量。
It has millions of weekly downloads.

208
00:20:37,902 --> 00:20:42,308
攻击者成功将恶意版本的 NX 发布到了 NPM 上。
Attackers were able to get malicious versions of it published to NPM.

209
00:20:42,926 --> 00:20:53,956
这些包仅上线了大约四个小时，但在那段时间内，又有超过一千个系统被攻破，并窃取了 2300 多个凭据。
Now these packages were only live for about four hours, but in that window, more than a thousand systems were compromised with over 2300 credentials harvested.

210
00:20:54,894 --> 00:21:01,220
我不会花太多时间去解释这个事件中供应链攻击部分是如何实施的。
Now I'm not going to spend much time on how the supply chain compromise part of the incident got pulled off.

211
00:21:01,710 --> 00:21:07,428
我想重点关注的是，在被污染的包进入开发者的机器后发生了什么。
What I want to focus on is what happened after the poisoned package landed on the developer's machine.

212
00:21:08,430 --> 00:21:13,252
因为那个安装后脚本本身并没有尝试窃取任何东西。
Because the Post install script didn't try to steal anything itself.

213
00:21:14,382 --> 00:21:27,312
它包含的内容只是直接发给开发者自己的 AI 编程助手的提示词，要求它在本地机器上寻找密钥，并把它们推送到公共仓库中
All it contained was a prompt addressed directly to the developer's own AI coding assistant, and it told them to go find secrets on the local machine and push them to a public repository

214
00:21:27,472 --> 00:21:30,948
这样攻击者稍后就可以过来把它们拿走。
so the attackers can swing by later and just grab them up.

215
00:21:31,822 --> 00:21:41,060
现在，由于这些开发工具已经是受信任的本地工具，它们已经拥有了所有权限，拥有完整的系统文件访问权限，
Now, because these dev coding tools were already trusted local tools, they already had all the permissions, they had full file system access,

216
00:21:41,614 --> 00:21:47,908
这使得它成了那台机器上最有效的搜索工具，而且正处于随时可能被利用的状态。
that made it the single most effective search tool that was already sitting on that machine, ready to be corrupted.

217
00:21:49,454 --> 00:21:57,380
现在，这次事件的核心在于恶意行为者能够利用一个受信任的依赖项，直接与一个未沙箱化的智能体进行通信。
Now, at the core of this incident is that a malicious actor was able to use a trusted dependency to talk directly to an unsandboxed agent.

218
00:21:58,272 --> 00:22:07,952
而智能体运行的环境——也就是这台本地笔记本电脑——包含了很多值得窃取的东西，从 SSH 密钥到云端凭据，
And the environment that the agent was running on, the local laptop, held a lot of things that was worth stealing from SSH keys to cloud credentials,

219
00:22:08,112 --> 00:22:12,580
还有开发者使用编程智能体去操作过的每一个服务的令牌。
tokens for every service that the developer used a coding agent to touch.

